```
using var stream = new FileStream("data.db", FileMode.OpenOrCreate);
using var db = new LiteDatabase(stream);            // non-MemoryStream, writable -> _checkpointOverride = 1000
db.BeginTrans();
db.GetCollection<Order>().Insert(order);
throw new InvalidOperationException("business rule failed");   // or simply forget Commit()
```
At the end of the `using`, `Dispose()` calls `_engine.Pragma(CHECKPOINT, 1000)`, `_locker.IsInTransaction` is true on this thread, and a `LiteException` ("The current thread already contains an open transaction...") is thrown *from Dispose*. It replaces the caller's original exception, and `_engine.Dispose()` never runs, so the disk service, its background writer, the sort temp file and the lock service leak for the lifetime of the process, the data file's exclusive handle is never released, and any commit whose eager `TryCheckpoint()` was skipped (WalIndexService.TryCheckpoint returns 0 whenever another thread has a read lock) is silently discarded with the in-memory log.

```
// DI-registered singleton LiteDatabase
using var db = new LiteDatabase("app.db");

using (var repo = new LiteRepository(db))      // scoped per request
{
    repo.Insert(order);
}                                               // <- disposes the shared singleton db

db.GetCollection<Order>().FindAll().ToList();  // LiteException: engine is disposed
```
One scoped repository silently closes the application-wide database; every later operation on `db` — and every other repository built over it — fails. `new LiteRepository((ILiteDatabase)null)` likewise NREs at the first member access rather than at construction.

```
class Person { public int Id { get; set; } public int Age { get; set; } }
// collection already holds documents written before `Age` existed (schema evolution),
// or a row inserted via db.GetCollection("people") as BsonDocument without "Age"
db.GetCollection("people").Insert(new BsonDocument { ["_id"] = 1 });
db.GetCollection("people").Insert(new BsonDocument { ["_id"] = 2, ["Age"] = 30 });

db.GetCollection<Person>("people").Min(x => x.Age);   // -> NullReferenceException
```
Expected 0 (pre-regression behaviour) or a typed error; instead an unhandled NullReferenceException escapes a read-only query. `Max<K>` (line 200) has the same shape whenever the highest-sorting value is null, i.e. when *every* document lacks the field.

